description:
auth.rs:62-71 — pub struct CustomCaTransport { der_cert: Vec<u8> }

API surface: single CA cert, DER-encoded only, single root.

Real-world deployments routinely need:
1. **PEM input.** Operators have PEM files (text-format CA certs). Today every caller writes:
     let pem = std::fs::read_to_string("ca.pem")?;
     let der = rustls_pemfile::certs(&mut pem.as_bytes())?.into_iter().next()?;
     let transport = CustomCaTransport::new(der);
   Every caller. Same boilerplate. The crate could ship a CustomCaTransport::from_pem() helper.

2. **Multi-cert chains.** A private PKI typically has a root + intermediate. CustomCaTransport stores ONE DER cert. There is no way to add an intermediate. Workaround: caller has to construct their own reqwest::ClientBuilder, add multiple certs, then... they can't, because TransportConfig doesn't expose a way to inject a pre-built reqwest::Client.

3. **No client cert support.** Mutual TLS (mTLS) requires the client to present a cert. CustomCaTransport only configures the trust root. A caller wanting mTLS has to roll their own TransportConfig impl from scratch — at which point why does CustomCaTransport exist at all?

The naming claim "Custom CA transport" implies it handles all custom-CA cases. It handles exactly one: single-root, DER-encoded, no client cert. This is defensive-code-forcing: every real-world consumer with anything more complex writes their own TransportConfig and the foundation type is decorative.

Recommendation: in a minor release, add a builder:
  pub struct CustomTransportBuilder {
      roots: Vec<Vec<u8>>,
      client_certs: Option<Vec<u8>>,
      client_key: Option<Vec<u8>>,
      timeouts: ...,
  }
  pub fn from_pem_file(path: &Path) -> Result<Self, ...>;
  pub fn from_pem_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Self, ...>;
  pub fn build(self) -> impl TransportConfig;

The single-cert CustomCaTransport stays for backward compat.
